/* Kenya Grade 10 Physics 1.1 — additional concept depth pack.
   Append-only content; preserves the KICD-aligned chapter and existing assessments. */
(()=> {
 const list=window.CBE_COUNTRY_NOTES?.Kenya?.levels?.["Grade 10"]?.Physics;
 const c=list?.find(x=>x.id==="ke-g10-phy-11-intro");
 if(!c||c.__introDepthPack20261116) return;
 c.__introDepthPack20261116=true;
 c.sections=c.sections||[];
 c.sections.push(
 {heading:"Physics as a quantitative science: models, laws and explanations",paragraphs:[
 "Physics is concerned with the fundamental rules that describe matter, energy, space, time and interactions. A physical model is a simplified representation of a real system. A model may use a diagram, equation, graph, scale drawing or computer simulation. The model deliberately leaves out details that are not important to the question being investigated. For example, when calculating the motion of a ball over a short distance, air resistance may be ignored if its effect is very small compared with the forces being considered. The assumption must be stated because it defines the conditions under which the model is useful.",
 "A scientific law describes a consistent relationship observed in nature, often in mathematical form. A theory is a well-supported explanatory framework that accounts for a wide range of evidence. Neither is simply a guess. A model or theory can be refined when new measurements reveal limitations. Newton's laws explain many everyday motions, while relativity is needed for very high speeds and strong gravitational fields. The existence of a more general theory does not make a useful model worthless; it clarifies the range within which that model works accurately.",
 "A good physical explanation connects an observation to a mechanism and, where possible, to a measurable relationship. Saying that an object falls because it is heavy is incomplete: gravitational force acts on all objects with mass, and in the absence of significant air resistance objects near Earth's surface have the same gravitational acceleration. Scientific explanations should identify the relevant variables, state assumptions and distinguish evidence from opinion."
 ]},
 {heading:"Physical quantities, SI units and dimensional consistency",paragraphs:[
 "A physical quantity is a property that can be measured and expressed as a number multiplied by a unit. The International System of Units provides a common language for measurements. The seven SI base quantities are length, mass, time, electric current, thermodynamic temperature, amount of substance and luminous intensity. Many other quantities are derived from them. Speed is measured in metres per second, acceleration in metres per second squared, force in newtons and energy in joules.",
 "Dimensional analysis checks whether an equation is consistent with the quantities it relates. For example, distance equals speed multiplied by time: metres per second multiplied by seconds gives metres. In the equation for force, F = ma, kilograms multiplied by metres per second squared gives the newton. Dimensional consistency is necessary but not sufficient to prove an equation correct; a dimensionally consistent equation may still have an incorrect numerical factor or physical assumption.",
 "Prefixes make very large and very small measurements easier to write. A kilometre is 1,000 metres, a centimetre is one hundredth of a metre, and a millimetre is one thousandth of a metre. Scientific notation expresses a value as a number between 1 and 10 multiplied by a power of ten. Learners should convert units before substituting into equations, especially when centimetres, kilometres, minutes or grams appear alongside SI units."
 ]},
 {heading:"Scalars and vectors: magnitude, direction and representation",paragraphs:[
 "A scalar quantity is described by magnitude alone. Examples include mass, time, temperature, distance, speed, energy and density. A vector quantity has both magnitude and direction. Examples include displacement, velocity, acceleration, force and momentum. The distinction matters because vectors cannot always be combined by ordinary arithmetic. Two forces of 5 N acting in opposite directions have a resultant of zero, not 10 N.",
 "A vector can be represented by an arrow. The arrow length represents magnitude according to a chosen scale, and the arrowhead indicates direction. For vectors along one straight line, choose a positive direction and assign signs consistently. If east is positive, a displacement of 8 m east is +8 m and a displacement of 3 m west is −3 m. Their resultant is +5 m, meaning 5 m east. The sign communicates direction relative to the chosen axis; it is not a negative physical length.",
 "For perpendicular vectors, a right-angled triangle can be used. If a learner walks 3 m east and then 4 m north, the resultant displacement is the hypotenuse: √(3² + 4²) = 5 m. Its direction can be found using trigonometry. The total distance travelled is 7 m, while the displacement is 5 m in a north-east direction. Distance and displacement answer different questions and should not be confused."
 ]},
 {heading:"Measurement uncertainty, precision and significant figures",paragraphs:[
 "Every measurement has uncertainty because instruments have finite resolution and observations are affected by the method used. A ruler with millimetre divisions cannot normally justify a reading to a fraction of a micrometre. The uncertainty should be consistent with the instrument and procedure. Repeating a measurement helps reveal random variation, while calibration and zero checks help identify systematic error.",
 "Random errors cause readings to vary unpredictably around a typical value. Repeated readings can be summarised using a mean, and the spread can be described using a range. Systematic errors shift readings in a consistent direction, perhaps because a balance has a zero error or a stopwatch runs slow. Repeating the same flawed procedure does not remove a systematic error. The instrument or method must be checked and corrected.",
 "Significant figures communicate the precision of a reported value. A calculated answer should not imply greater precision than the measurements justify. For multiplication and division, the result is commonly rounded to the same number of significant figures as the least precise measured input. For addition and subtraction, decimal places are considered. In practical reports, retain extra digits during intermediate calculations and round the final result appropriately."
 ]},
 {heading:"Graphs in Physics: choosing axes, gradients and intercepts",paragraphs:[
 "Graphs reveal relationships between physical quantities. The independent variable is usually placed on the horizontal axis and the dependent variable on the vertical axis. Each axis needs a quantity name and unit, and the scale should use most of the available graph area. Data points should be plotted accurately. A line of best fit should represent the overall pattern rather than join every point in a zigzag.",
 "The gradient is the change in the vertical quantity divided by the change in the horizontal quantity. Its units are the vertical-axis units divided by the horizontal-axis units. On a distance-time graph, gradient represents speed; on a velocity-time graph, gradient represents acceleration. The area under a velocity-time graph represents displacement. These interpretations follow from the definitions of the quantities and the geometry of the graph.",
 "An intercept may have physical meaning or may reveal an offset in measurement. A straight line through the origin suggests direct proportionality, but the conclusion should be based on evidence and the uncertainty of the measurements. A curve may indicate a changing rate or a non-linear relationship. Learners should not force a straight line through curved data simply because a linear relationship is easier to calculate."
 ]},
 {heading:"Energy, work and power: a connected framework",paragraphs:[
 "Energy is a scalar quantity that can be transferred between objects or transformed from one form to another. Common forms include kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, chemical, thermal, electrical, light and sound energy. The principle of conservation of energy states that energy is not created or destroyed in an isolated system; it is transferred or transformed. In everyday processes some energy is dissipated to the surroundings, often as thermal energy, making it less available for useful work.",
 "Work is done when a force causes displacement in its direction. For a constant force acting parallel to displacement, work done equals force multiplied by distance. The SI unit is the joule, equivalent to a newton metre. If the force is perpendicular to the displacement, it does no work on the object in the direction of that displacement. This helps explain why carrying a stationary load at a constant height involves effort by the person but no mechanical work by the upward force on the load during purely horizontal motion.",
 "Power is the rate of energy transfer or work done. One watt is one joule per second. Two machines may perform the same work but have different power ratings if one completes the task in less time. Efficiency compares useful output energy or power with total input and is usually expressed as a percentage. Efficiency is below 100 percent in real devices because some energy is transferred to unwanted forms or surroundings."
 ]},
 {heading:"Forces, equilibrium and free-body diagrams",paragraphs:[
 "A force is an interaction that can change an object's motion or shape. Forces are vectors and may act through contact, such as friction and tension, or at a distance, such as gravitational and magnetic forces. The resultant force is the vector sum of all forces acting on an object. If the resultant force is zero, the object has no acceleration: it may remain at rest or continue moving at constant velocity.",
 "A free-body diagram isolates one object and shows the external forces acting on it. The object can be represented by a simple box or dot, with arrows labelled for weight, normal contact force, tension, friction, drag or applied force as appropriate. The arrows should point in the direction in which each force acts on the selected object. A common mistake is to include forces that the object exerts on something else; those belong on the free-body diagram of the other object.",
 "For an object resting on a horizontal table, its weight acts downward and the normal contact force from the table acts upward. If there is no vertical acceleration, the two forces have equal magnitude. The normal force is not always equal to weight: on a slope or in a lift accelerating vertically, the balance differs. Diagrams help learners identify the forces before choosing equations."
 ]},
 {heading:"Pressure and density as links between force, area and matter",paragraphs:[
 "Density is mass per unit volume. It describes how much mass is packed into a given space. The SI unit is kilograms per cubic metre, although grams per cubic centimetre is also common in laboratory work. For a uniform substance, density equals mass divided by volume. A sample's average density can be calculated even when it contains pores or spaces, but the method used to measure its volume must be stated.",
 "Pressure is force per unit area when the force acts normally on a surface. The SI unit is the pascal, equal to one newton per square metre. For the same force, reducing the contact area increases pressure. This explains why a sharp blade cuts more effectively than a blunt one and why broad straps reduce pressure on a shoulder. The direction and distribution of force matter in real contact situations.",
 "Density and pressure help explain floating and sinking, building foundations, hydraulic systems and fluid behaviour. A body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force because fluid pressure increases with depth. The pressure difference between the lower and upper surfaces produces an upward resultant. The buoyant force equals the weight of displaced fluid in the standard Archimedes principle model."
 ]},
 {heading:"Thermal physics and particle explanations",paragraphs:[
 "Temperature describes the thermal state of a system and is related to the average random kinetic energy of its particles in a simple particle model. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference; it is not a substance stored inside an object. Thermal energy can be transferred by conduction, convection and radiation. These mechanisms often operate together, but one may dominate in a particular situation.",
 "In conduction, energy is transferred through interactions between neighbouring particles and, in metals, mobile electrons. Convection occurs in fluids when warmer, less dense regions rise and cooler, denser regions sink, setting up circulation. Radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves and does not require a material medium. Dark, matt surfaces are generally good absorbers and emitters of thermal radiation, while shiny surfaces are generally poorer absorbers and emitters.",
 "Changes of state involve energy transfer without a temperature change during the phase transition of a pure substance at constant pressure. During melting or boiling, energy is used to change the arrangement and separation of particles rather than increase their average kinetic energy. This explains latent heat. A temperature-time graph can show flat sections during phase changes and sloping sections when temperature changes within a single state."
 ]},
 {heading:"Physics in technology, health, transport and environmental decisions",paragraphs:[
 "Physics underpins medical technologies such as ultrasound, X-ray imaging, magnetic resonance imaging and radiation therapy. Each uses different physical principles and has benefits and risks. X-rays are ionising radiation and require controlled exposure; ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves and is widely used for imaging soft tissues. A responsible explanation considers both the mechanism and the safety measures used in practice.",
 "Transport systems use mechanics, energy transfer and materials science. Seat belts and airbags increase the time over which a passenger's momentum changes during a collision, reducing the average force for a given change in momentum. Tyres need suitable friction, while brakes convert kinetic energy mainly into thermal energy. Vehicle efficiency depends on engine or motor design, aerodynamic drag, rolling resistance, mass and driving conditions.",
 "Environmental applications include solar cells, wind turbines, hydroelectric generation, efficient lighting, insulation and energy storage. Comparing technologies requires more than naming a renewable source. Learners should consider energy conversion, reliability, storage, location, environmental effects, construction materials and life-cycle costs. Physics provides quantitative tools for comparing power output, efficiency and energy demand so that decisions are based on evidence."
 ]}
 );
})();
